\title{A Radix-Growth Threshold:\\ When Is the Height of a Self-Escalating Positional System $O(\lgs n)$?}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 23, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study generalized positional numeral systems whose radix at each position is
determined by the weight already accumulated. Given a \emph{radix schedule}
$r:\NN\to\NN$, define weights by $V_0=1$ and $V_{k+1}=r(V_k)V_k$, and define the
\emph{radix height} of $n$ to be $K_r(n)=\min\{k: n<V_k\}$, the number of digit
positions required to represent $n$. We determine exactly when $K_r$ is
$O(\lgs n)$, where $\lgs$ is the iterated binary logarithm.

Three results are established. First, a \emph{dichotomy}: if $r(x)\ge 2$
everywhere and $2^x\le r(x)$ for all $x\ge x_0$, then $K_r(n)\le x_0+\lgs n+1$
for every $n$ --- an $O(\lgs)$ bound with additive, not multiplicative,
overhead; whereas if $r$ is monotone with $r\ge 2$ and $r(x)\le x^C$ for a fixed
$C$ and all large $x$, then for every constant $c$ and every bound $N$ there is
an input $n\ge N$ with $c(\lgs n+1)<K_r(n)$, so $K_r$ is not $O(\lgs n)$.
Second, \emph{sharpness}: for schedules bounded above by $2^x$ one has
$\lgs n\le 2K_r(n)$, so the canonical schedule $r(x)=\max(2,2^x)$ satisfies
$\tfrac12\lgs n\le K_r(n)\le \lgs n+1$ and its radix height is genuinely
$\Theta(\lgs n)$. Third, \emph{structure}: the polynomial hypothesis is
inessential. We isolate a master transfer principle showing that sublinear
growth of $k\mapsto \lgs(V_k)$ alone defeats the $O(\lgs)$ bound, deduce that
weights bounded by \emph{any fixed height} of iterated exponentials of a linear
function of $k$ already fail, and finally characterize the fast regime
intrinsically: $K_r(n)=O(\lgs n)$ if and only if the weights overtake the tower
of twos along an arithmetic subsequence, $\exists c\,\forall k,\ T_k\le
V_{c(k+1)}$.

The threshold is therefore not located at ``polynomial versus exponential'' but
at the \emph{height of the iterated-exponential tower} under which the weights
live: bounded height loses, growing height wins.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} mixed-radix representation, iterated logarithm,
tower of twos, growth rates, positional numeral systems, hierarchical scales,
asymptotic dichotomy.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

A positional numeral system is determined by its sequence of place weights. In
base $b$ the weights are $1,b,b^2,\dots$ and representing $n$ costs
$\Theta(\log n)$ digits. In a \emph{mixed-radix} system one fixes bases
$b_0,b_1,\dots$ and takes $V_0=1$, $V_{k+1}=b_kV_k$; time and calendar systems
are of this type, as are factorial number systems ($b_k=k+2$) and combinatorial
ranking schemes.

Physical and computational hierarchies suggest a further generalization in which
the base at position $k$ is a function of the weight already reached. This is
the natural formalization of a self-similar stratification whose coarsening
factor is governed by the coarseness already attained: a lattice refinement
scheme, a renormalization ladder, a nested unit system. It is likewise the
natural formalization of a recursive escalation whose next stage size is
dictated by the current one.

Concretely, we fix a \emph{radix schedule} $r:\NN\to\NN$ and set
\[
V_0=1,\qquad V_{k+1}=r(V_k)\,V_k .
\]
Constant $r\equiv b$ recovers base $b$. The schedule $r(x)=2^x$ produces an
explosive system with weights $1,2,8,2048,2^{2059},\dots$. The schedule
$r(x)=x^2+2$ produces a system growing doubly exponentially in $k$, which is
fast by any ordinary measure yet, as we prove, still on the slow side of the
threshold.

The quantity of interest is the number of digit positions needed,
\[
K_r(n)=\min\{k\in\NN : n<V_k\}.
\]

\subsection{The iterated logarithm and the tower}

The relevant yardstick is the iterated binary logarithm
\[
\lgs n=\begin{cases}0,& n\le 1,\\ 1+\lgs\big(\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor\big),& n>1,\end{cases}
\]
which terminates because $\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor<n$ for $n\ge 1$. Its inverse is
the tower of twos $T_0=1$, $T_{k+1}=2^{T_k}$, with $T_1=2$, $T_2=4$, $T_3=16$,
$T_4=65536$, $T_5=2^{65536}$.

\subsection{Statement of the main results}

A schedule $r$ is \emph{admissible} if $r(x)\ge 2$ for all $x$. We prove:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \textbf{Theorem A (exponential regime).} If $r$ is admissible and
$2^x\le r(x)$ for all $x\ge x_0$, then $K_r(n)\le x_0+\lgs n+1$ for all $n$.
\item \textbf{Theorem B (sharpness).} If $r$ is admissible and $r(x)\le 2^x$ for
all $x\ge 1$, then $\lgs n\le 2K_r(n)$. Consequently $r(x)=\max(2,2^x)$ has
$\tfrac12\lgs n\le K_r(n)\le \lgs n+1$.
\item \textbf{Theorem C (polynomial regime).} If $r$ is admissible, monotone and
$r(x)\le x^C$ for all $x\ge x_0$, then for every $c,N$ there is $n\ge N$ with
$c(\lgs n+1)<K_r(n)$.
\item \textbf{Theorem D (master transfer principle).} If $r$ is admissible and
$\lgs(V_k)\le h(k)$ for a weakly sublinear $h$, the conclusion of Theorem C holds.
\item \textbf{Theorem E (fixed-height theorem).} If $r$ is admissible and
$V_k\le E^{(h)}(M+Ek)$ for a fixed height $h$ and $M,E\ge 2$, then $K_r$ is not
$O(\lgs n)$.
\item \textbf{Theorem F (characterization).} For admissible $r$:
$\exists c\,\forall n,\ K_r(n)\le c(\lgs n+1)$ iff
$\exists c\,\forall k,\ T_k\le V_{c(k+1)}$.
\end{itemize}

Theorem F is the conceptual endpoint: since $\lgs$ inverts the tower, the fast
regime is precisely the regime in which the weights track a tower after a linear
reindexing.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Radix schedule and weights]
A \emph{radix schedule} is a function $r:\NN\to\NN$. Its \emph{weight sequence}
is $V_0=1$, $V_{k+1}=r(V_k)V_k$. A schedule is \emph{admissible} if $r(x)\ge 2$
for all $x$, and \emph{monotone} if $x\le y\Rightarrow r(x)\le r(y)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Radix height]
For admissible $r$ and $n\in\NN$, $K_r(n)=\min\{k: n<V_k\}$. The minimum is over
a nonempty set (Lemma~\ref{lem:exists}).
\end{definition}

The interpretation is standard: writing $n$ with digit
$d_k\in\{0,\dots,r(V_k)-1\}$ at position $k$ uses exactly positions
$0,\dots,K_r(n)-1$, since positions below $k$ represent all residues modulo
$V_k$ and no more.

\begin{definition}[Tower and iterated exponential]
$T_0=1$, $T_{k+1}=2^{T_k}$. More generally $E^{(0)}(x)=x$ and
$E^{(h+1)}(x)=2^{E^{(h)}(x)}$, so $T_k=E^{(k)}(1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Iterated logarithm]
$\lgs n=0$ for $n\le 1$ and $\lgs n=1+\lgs\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor$ for $n>1$,
where $\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor$ is the largest $j$ with $2^j\le n$ (and $0$ for
$n=0$).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The $O(\lgs)$ property]
$K_r$ \emph{is $O(\lgs n)$} if there is $c$ with $K_r(n)\le c(\lgs n+1)$ for all
$n$. The additive $+1$ is the correct normalization: $\lgs n=0$ for $n\le 1$
while $K_r(n)\ge 1$ there, so without it no schedule would qualify.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical schedules]
$\mathrm{expsched}(x)=\max(2,2^x)$ and, for an exponent $C$,
$\mathrm{polysched}_C(x)=x^C+2$.
\end{definition}

\section{The calculus of $\lgs$ and the tower}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
$\lgs$ is monotone: $m\le n\Rightarrow \lgs m\le\lgs n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $n$. If $m\le 1$ the left side is $0$. Otherwise
$1<m\le n$, and both sides unfold as $1+\lgs\lfloor\log_2\cdot\rfloor$; the
integer logarithm is monotone and strictly decreasing on inputs $>1$, so the
induction hypothesis applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Domination by a single logarithm]\label{lem:loglog}
$\lgs n\le\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor$ for all $n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $n\le 1$ both sides vanish. For $n>1$ put $\ell=\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor\ge 1$;
then $\lgs n=1+\lgs\ell\le 1+\lfloor\log_2\ell\rfloor\le\ell$ by induction and
$\lfloor\log_2\ell\rfloor<\ell$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Peeling one exponential]\label{lem:peel}
If $a\ge 1$ then $\lgs(2^a)=1+\lgs a$; for all $a\ge 0$, $\lgs(2^a)\le 1+\lgs a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:peel}
If $n\le 2^a$ then $\lgs n\le 1+\lgs a$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed height shifts $\lgs$ by a constant]\label{lem:height}
For all $h,y$: $\lgs\big(E^{(h)}(y)\big)\le h+\lgs y$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $h$ using Lemma~\ref{lem:peel}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[$\lgs$ inverts the tower]\label{lem:invert}
$T_k\ge 1$ for all $k$, and $\lgs(T_j)=j$ for all $j$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\lgs(T_{j+1})=\lgs(2^{T_j})=1+\lgs(T_j)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:peel}, valid since
$T_j\ge 1$; induct.
\end{proof}

In particular $\lgs$ is unbounded, so none of the statements below is vacuous.

\begin{lemma}[Tower characterization]\label{lem:towerchar}
For all $n$: $n<T_{\lgs n+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Strong induction. For $n\le 1$: $T_1=2>n$. For $n>1$ let
$\ell=\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor$. By induction $\ell<T_{\lgs\ell+1}$, hence
$\ell+1\le T_{\lgs\ell+1}$ and
\[
n<2^{\ell+1}\le 2^{T_{\lgs\ell+1}}=T_{\lgs\ell+2}=T_{\lgs n+1},
\]
using $\lgs n=1+\lgs\ell$.
\end{proof}

Lemmas~\ref{lem:invert} and \ref{lem:towerchar} say: $\lgs n$ is, up to $\pm1$,
the height of the shortest tower exceeding $n$. Every subsequent argument uses
only this.

\section{Basic control on weights and radix height}

Fix an admissible schedule $r$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:twopow}
$2^k\le V_k$ for all $k$; in particular $V_k\ge 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Strict growth]\label{lem:strict}
$V$ is monotone and strictly increasing: $V_k<V_{k+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$V_{k+1}=r(V_k)V_k\ge 2V_k>V_k$ since $V_k\ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Existence]\label{lem:exists}
For every $n$ there is $k$ with $n<V_k$; indeed $k=n$ works, since
$n<2^n\le V_n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Defining properties of $K_r$]\label{lem:defprop}
For all $n,k$:
(i) $n<V_{K_r(n)}$;
(ii) if $n<V_k$ then $K_r(n)\le k$;
(iii) if $V_k\le n$ then $k<K_r(n)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
(i),(ii) are the definition of a minimum with Lemma~\ref{lem:exists}. (iii): if
$K_r(n)\le k$ then $V_{K_r(n)}\le V_k\le n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:strict},
contradicting (i).
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity and jump points]\label{lem:jump}
$K_r$ is monotone, and $K_r(V_k)=k+1$ for every $k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Monotonicity: $m\le n<V_{K_r(n)}$ gives $K_r(m)\le K_r(n)$. Jump points:
$V_k<V_{k+1}$ gives $K_r(V_k)\le k+1$ by \ref{lem:defprop}(ii), and
$V_k\le V_k$ gives $k<K_r(V_k)$ by \ref{lem:defprop}(iii).
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:jump} is the crucial \emph{probe} used in every lower bound: at
$n=V_k$ the radix height is known exactly, so the only remaining task is to
bound $\lgs(V_k)$.

\section{The exponential regime}

\begin{lemma}[Weights dominate a shifted tower]\label{lem:towerle}
Let $r$ be admissible with $2^x\le r(x)$ for all $x\ge x_0$. Then
$T_j\le V_{x_0+j}$ for all $j$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $j$. For $j=0$, $T_0=1\le V_{x_0}$. Assume $T_j\le V_{x_0+j}$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:twopow}, $V_{x_0+j}\ge 2^{x_0+j}>x_0+j\ge x_0$, so the hypothesis
applies at $x=V_{x_0+j}$:
\[
T_{j+1}=2^{T_j}\le 2^{V_{x_0+j}}\le r(V_{x_0+j})\le r(V_{x_0+j})V_{x_0+j}=V_{x_0+j+1}. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A: exponential regime]\label{thm:A}
Let $r$ be admissible with $2^x\le r(x)$ for all $x\ge x_0$. Then for every $n$,
\[
K_r(n)\le x_0+\lgs n+1 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:towerchar}, $n<T_{\lgs n+1}$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:towerle},
$T_{\lgs n+1}\le V_{x_0+\lgs n+1}$. Apply \ref{lem:defprop}(ii).
\end{proof}

The bound has \emph{no multiplicative constant}: the whole cost of the
hypothesis holding only beyond $x_0$ is an additive $x_0$.

\section{Sharpness}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:elem}
For $v\ge 1$, $2v\le 2^v$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Weights trapped under a doubled tower]\label{lem:trap}
Let $r$ be admissible with $r(x)\le 2^x$ for all $x\ge 1$. Then
$V_k\le T_{2k}$ for all $k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction; $V_0=1=T_0$. Writing $v=V_k\ge 1$ and assuming $v\le T_{2k}$,
\[
V_{k+1}=r(v)v\le 2^v\cdot 2^v=2^{2v}\le 2^{2^v}\le 2^{2^{T_{2k}}}=T_{2k+2}=T_{2(k+1)},
\]
using $v<2^v$, Lemma~\ref{lem:elem} and monotonicity of $x\mapsto 2^x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[B: matching lower bound]\label{thm:B}
Let $r$ be admissible with $r(x)\le 2^x$ for all $x\ge 1$. Then
$\lgs n\le 2K_r(n)$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$n<V_{K_r(n)}\le T_{2K_r(n)}$ by \ref{lem:defprop}(i) and \ref{lem:trap}. Apply
$\lgs$ (monotone, Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}) and use $\lgs(T_j)=j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[$\Theta(\lgs)$ for the canonical schedule]\label{cor:theta}
For $r(x)=\max(2,2^x)$ and every $n$,
\[
\tfrac12\lgs n\;\le\; K_r(n)\;\le\;\lgs n+1 .
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$r(x)\ge 2$ and $r(x)\ge 2^x$ everywhere, so Theorem~\ref{thm:A} applies with
$x_0=0$; and $r(x)\le 2^x$ for $x\ge1$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:B} applies.
\end{proof}

\section{The slow side: transfer principles}

\subsection{What actually matters}

Evaluate the comparison $c(\lgs n+1)$ versus $K_r(n)$ at $n=V_k$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:jump}, $K_r(V_k)=k+1$ exactly, so the comparison becomes
\[
c\big(\lgs(V_k)+1\big)\;\overset{?}{<}\;k+1,
\]
a purely arithmetic question about $k\mapsto\lgs(V_k)$.

\begin{definition}[Weakly sublinear]
$h:\NN\to\NN$ is \emph{weakly sublinear} if for all $c,N$ there is $k\ge N$ with
$c(h(k)+1)<k+1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[D: master transfer principle]\label{thm:D}
Let $r$ be admissible and suppose $\lgs(V_k)\le h(k)$ for all $k$, with $h$
weakly sublinear. Then for all $c,N$ there is $n\ge N$ with
$c(\lgs n+1)<K_r(n)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Given $c,N$ choose $k\ge N$ with $c(h(k)+1)<k+1$ and take $n=V_k$. Then
$n\ge 2^k>k\ge N$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:twopow}, and $K_r(n)\ge k+1$ by
\ref{lem:defprop}(iii). Finally
$c(\lgs n+1)\le c(h(k)+1)<k+1\le K_r(n)$.
\end{proof}

The witnesses are the weights themselves, and since $V_k\ge 2^k$ they are
arbitrarily large: the failure is asymptotic, not a small-$n$ artefact.

\subsection{Fixed tower height is fatal}

\begin{lemma}[Doubly exponential is two exponentials of a linear function]\label{lem:dbl}
For all $M,E,k$: $M^{E^k}\le E^{(2)}(M+Ek)=2^{2^{M+Ek}}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Using $x<2^x$: $M^{E^k}\le(2^M)^{E^k}=2^{ME^k}$ and
$ME^k\le 2^M(2^E)^k=2^{M+Ek}$; combine and apply monotonicity of $2^{(\cdot)}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Logarithm of a linear function is weakly sublinear]\label{lem:slow}
For fixed $a$ and $M,E\ge 2$, $h(k)=a+\lfloor\log_2(M+Ek)\rfloor$ is weakly
sublinear.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Given $c,N$, pick $t\ge\max(4c,\,4(M+E+a+1),\,N,\,1)$ and set $m=2t$, $k=2^m$;
then $k>m\ge N$. Since $M+Ek\le 2^M2^E2^m=2^{M+E+m}$, we get
$\lfloor\log_2(M+Ek)\rfloor\le M+E+m$, hence
\[
c\,(h(k)+1)\le c\big((M+E+a+1)+m\big)\le \tfrac{t}{4}\Big(\tfrac{t}{4}+2t\Big)
=\tfrac{9}{16}t^2\le t^2<2^{t}2^{t}=2^{2t}=k<k+1,
\]
using $c\le t/4$, $M+E+a+1\le t/4$, $m=2t$ and $t<2^t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[E: fixed-height theorem]\label{thm:E}
Let $r$ be admissible and suppose there are a \emph{fixed} height $h$ and
constants $M,E\ge 2$ with $V_k\le E^{(h)}(M+Ek)$ for all $k$. Then for all $c,N$
there is $n\ge N$ with $c(\lgs n+1)<K_r(n)$; in particular $K_r$ is not
$O(\lgs n)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemmas~\ref{lem:mono}, \ref{lem:height} and \ref{lem:loglog},
\[
\lgs(V_k)\le\lgs\big(E^{(h)}(M+Ek)\big)\le h+\lgs(M+Ek)\le h+\lfloor\log_2(M+Ek)\rfloor,
\]
weakly sublinear by Lemma~\ref{lem:slow}. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:D}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Doubly exponential weights]\label{cor:dbl}
If $r$ is admissible and $V_k\le M^{E^k}$ with $M,E\ge 2$, then $K_r$ is not
$O(\lgs n)$.
\end{corollary}

What kills the bound is not slow growth in any everyday sense --- $E^{(3)}(M+Ek)$
is astronomically large --- but that the \emph{height} $h$ is constant.

\subsection{The polynomial regime}

\begin{lemma}[Globalizing a polynomial bound]\label{lem:global}
Let $r$ be monotone, admissible, with $r(x)\le x^C$ for all $x\ge x_0$. Then
$r(x)\le r(x_0)(x+1)^C$ for all $x$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[One step]\label{lem:step}
Under the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:global}, with $M=r(x_0)2^C$,
$V_{k+1}\le M\,V_k^{\,C+1}$ for all $k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$V_{k+1}=r(V_k)V_k\le r(x_0)(V_k+1)^CV_k\le r(x_0)(2V_k)^CV_k=MV_k^{C+1}$, using
$V_k\ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Doubly exponential weight bound]\label{prop:pp}
Under the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:global}, with $M=r(x_0)2^C\ge 2$ and
$E=C+2$: $V_k\le M^{E^k}$ for all $k$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
We prove $M V_k\le M^{E^k}$ by induction. At $k=0$: $M\cdot1=M^{E^0}$. Assuming
it at $k$, Lemma~\ref{lem:step} gives
\[
MV_{k+1}\le M\cdot MV_k^{C+1}=(M\cdot M)V_k^{C+1}\le M^{C+2}V_k^{C+2}
=(MV_k)^{C+2}\le\big(M^{E^k}\big)^{E}=M^{E^{k+1}} .
\]
Finally $V_k\le MV_k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[C: polynomial regime]\label{thm:C}
Let $r$ be monotone and admissible with $r(x)\le x^C$ for all $x\ge x_0$. Then
for every $c$ and every $N$ there is $n\ge N$ with $c(\lgs n+1)<K_r(n)$, and
consequently there is no constant $c$ with $K_r(n)\le c(\lgs n+1)$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:pp} gives $V_k\le M^{E^k}$ with $M=r(x_0)2^C\ge2$ and
$E=C+2\ge2$; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:dbl}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Radix-growth threshold]
Let $r$ be admissible with $2^x\le r(x)$ for $x\ge x_0$, and let $s$ be
monotone, admissible, with $s(x)\le x^C$ for $x\ge y_0$. Then
\[
\big(\forall n,\ K_r(n)\le x_0+\lgs n+1\big)\quad\text{and}\quad
\neg\,\exists c\;\forall n,\ K_s(n)\le c(\lgs n+1).
\]
\end{theorem}

\section{An intrinsic characterization}

\begin{theorem}[F: characterization of the fast regime]\label{thm:F}
Let $r$ be admissible. Then
\[
\exists c\;\forall n,\ K_r(n)\le c(\lgs n+1)
\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
\exists c\;\forall k,\ T_k\le V_{c(k+1)} .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Suppose $K_r(n)\le c(\lgs n+1)$ for all $n$. Fix $k$ and take
$n=T_k$; since $\lgs(T_k)=k$ we get $K_r(T_k)\le c(k+1)$. By
\ref{lem:defprop}(i), $T_k<V_{K_r(T_k)}$, and by monotonicity of $V$,
$V_{K_r(T_k)}\le V_{c(k+1)}$. Hence $T_k\le V_{c(k+1)}$.

($\Leftarrow$) Suppose $T_k\le V_{c(k+1)}$ for all $k$. Fix $n$ and put
$j=\lgs n$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:towerchar}, $n<T_{j+1}\le V_{c(j+2)}$, so
$K_r(n)\le c(j+2)$ by \ref{lem:defprop}(ii). Since $c(j+2)+cj=2c(j+1)$ we get
$K_r(n)\le 2c(\lgs n+1)$, so the constant $2c$ works.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:F} explains why $\lgs$, and no other slow function, appears:
since $\lgs$ inverts the tower, the class of schedules with $O(\lgs)$ height is
exactly the class whose weights track a tower up to a linear reparametrization
of the index. Theorem~\ref{thm:A} says super-exponential schedules satisfy the
right-hand condition; Theorem~\ref{thm:E} says a fixed-height bound is
incompatible with it; and Theorem~\ref{thm:F} shows there is no third
possibility, even for wild, non-monotone or oscillating schedules.

\section{Concrete instances}

\begin{example}[Canonical exponential schedule]
$r(x)=\max(2,2^x)$ is admissible with $2^x\le r(x)$ everywhere and $r(x)\le2^x$
for $x\ge1$. Its weights are $1,2,8,2048,2^{2048}\cdot2048,\dots$, and by
Corollary~\ref{cor:theta}, $\tfrac12\lgs n\le K_r(n)\le\lgs n+1$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Pure exponential with offset]
$r(x)=2^{x+1}$ is admissible with $2^x\le r(x)$ everywhere, so
$K_r(n)\le\lgs n+1$ for all $n$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[The polynomial family]
For each $C$, $\mathrm{polysched}_C(x)=x^C+2$ is admissible and monotone, and for
$x\ge3$ satisfies $x^C+2\le 3x^C\le x^{C+1}$. Theorem~\ref{thm:C} (exponent
$C+1$, threshold $3$) shows its radix height is not $O(\lgs n)$ --- for every
$C$. In particular $s(x)=x^2+2$ fails, although $V_k\approx V_{k-1}^3$ grows
doubly exponentially in $k$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Quasi-polynomial schedules]
$r(x)=2^{(\log_2 x)^C}$ exceeds every polynomial, yet its weights satisfy
$V_k\le E^{(2)}(M+Ek)$ for suitable constants, so Theorem~\ref{thm:E} applies.
This case is invisible to Theorem~\ref{thm:C} --- the payoff of separating the
transfer principle from the polynomial hypothesis.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Fixed base]
$r\equiv b$ with $b\ge2$ gives $V_k=b^k$ and $K_r(n)=\Theta(\log n)$: the
$h=1$, $E=b$ case of Theorem~\ref{thm:E}.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Weight tower with saturating arithmetic.}
Computing $K_r(n)$ needs the weights only up to the first exceeding $n$:
iterate $V\leftarrow r(V)V$ from $V=1$, stopping when $V>n$; this costs $K_r(n)$
iterations. For fast schedules $K_r(n)\le 6$ for any $n$ representable in a
computer. Intermediate weights may exceed machine representation, which is
harmless if one \emph{saturates}: replace any value exceeding $n$ by a sentinel,
since the loop terminates at the first such value.

\paragraph{Iterated logarithm via bit lengths.}
$\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor$ is the bit length of $n$ minus one, so $\lgs n$ is
computed by repeatedly replacing $n$ by its bit length minus one until reaching
$1$, counting the steps: $O(\lgs n)$ bit-length queries.

\paragraph{Certifying failure of the $O(\lgs)$ bound.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:D} tells us where to look: at the weights themselves. Compute
$V_k$ for increasing $k$, evaluate $R(k)=(k+1)/(\lgs(V_k)+1)$, and report the
least $k$ with $R(k)>c$. This yields an explicit witness $n=V_k$ with
$c(\lgs n+1)<K_r(n)$. For $r(x)=x^2+2$, $\lgs(V_k)$ equals $5$ over a very wide
range, so $R(k)$ grows essentially linearly and the search terminates at
$k\approx 5c$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Three growth scales, one threshold}
The subject is the interaction of the tower $T_k$ (height growing with the
index), the fixed-height iterated exponential $E^{(h)}(\text{linear})$, and the
iterated logarithm inverting the first. The boundary of the $O(\lgs)$ property
lies exactly between the first two, and the schedule matters only through which
side its weight sequence falls on. In most asymptotic questions the
polynomial/exponential divide is salient; here it is a red herring, since the
polynomial hypothesis already produces a \emph{doubly} exponential weight
sequence and the theorem is really about ``doubly'' being a constant.

\subsection{Hierarchical scales}
Multiscale descriptions --- renormalization ladders, adaptive mesh hierarchies,
nested unit systems, coarse-graining schemes --- are frequently specified by a
rule of the form ``the next scale factor is a function of the scale reached so
far''. The radix height is the number of strata needed to cover a target scale.
A coarsening rule that exponentiates reaches any target in a number of strata
bounded by an iterated logarithm plus a constant; a rule that raises the current
scale to a fixed power, however extravagant, provably does not.

\subsection{Iterated logarithms in algorithm analysis}
$\lgs$ is the characteristic complexity of union--find with path compression, of
certain range structures, and of deterministic coin-tossing colourings. The
present analysis says abstractly when this bound can appear: iterated-logarithmic
cost is available exactly when the recursion escalates by \emph{exponentiation},
and is destroyed by escalation of bounded exponential height. Theorem~\ref{thm:F}
shows the criterion is invariant under linear reparametrizations of depth.

\subsection{Strength and non-vacuity}
The negative results are proved in the strong form ``for every $c$ and every $N$
there is a witness $n\ge N$'', ruling out failure at finitely many inputs, and
they supply explicit witnesses. Both regimes are populated by explicit
schedules, and $\lgs$ is unbounded, so no hypothesis set here is empty.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{The intermediate zone.} The master transfer principle asks only that
$\lgs(V_k)$ be sublinear in $k$, so every schedule whose weights stay inside a
fixed-height tower falls on the slow side --- including $r(x)=2^{(\log_2 x)^C}$
and $r(x)=2^{2^{\log_2\log_2 x}}$. A complete map of the intermediate zone would
classify schedules whose weight towers have slowly \emph{growing} height, say
$V_k\le E^{(\alpha(k))}(k)$: for which $\alpha$ does $E^{(\alpha(k))}(k)$
dominate $T_{ck}$?

\paragraph{Second-order refinements.} Is the true asymptotic constant in
Corollary~\ref{cor:theta} equal to $1$? A finer analysis of the doubling in
Lemma~\ref{lem:trap} should determine the exact leading behaviour.

\paragraph{Non-monotone and randomized schedules.} Theorem~\ref{thm:F} needs
only admissibility, so it applies to oscillating schedules. For a random
schedule from a natural distribution, is the tower-tracking condition satisfied
almost surely or almost never?

\paragraph{Higher inverses.} Replacing the tower by the Ackermann hierarchy and
$\lgs$ by the inverse Ackermann function should yield an analogous threshold one
level higher; the proofs here are structurally generic.

\paragraph{Digit-level structure.} Only the \emph{number} of digits is studied
here. Digit distribution, carry statistics and arithmetic complexity in a
self-escalating system appear rich, since the alphabet at position $k$ has size
$r(V_k)$.

\paragraph{Constructive optimality.} Among admissible schedules with a
prescribed budget on $r$, which minimizes the radix height pointwise --- and
what changes under a total-information constraint weighting position $k$ by
$\log r(V_k)$?

\section{Summary}

For self-escalating positional systems with $V_0=1$, $V_{k+1}=r(V_k)V_k$ and
$K_r(n)=\min\{k:n<V_k\}$:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]
\item $2^x\le r(x)$ beyond $x_0$ implies $K_r(n)\le x_0+\lgs n+1$ for all $n$.
\item If additionally $r(x)\le2^x$ for $x\ge1$, then $\lgs n\le 2K_r(n)$; for
$r(x)=\max(2,2^x)$, $K_r(n)=\Theta(\lgs n)$.
\item Monotone $r$ with $r(x)\le x^C$ gives weights at most $M^{E^k}$ and hence
failure of $O(\lgs)$ on arbitrarily large inputs.
\item Failure follows from sublinearity of $k\mapsto\lgs(V_k)$ alone, hence from
\emph{any} fixed-height iterated-exponential bound. Polynomiality is incidental.
\item $K_r$ is $O(\lgs)$ if and only if $T_k\le V_{c(k+1)}$ for some $c$ and all
$k$: the weights must track the tower of twos up to a linear reindexing.
\end{enumerate}

